 matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= \\begin{bmatrix}7 & -11 & 10 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{3}:=R_{3} + R_{2} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -11 & 10 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd). Thus, $x_3$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we have $x_2=1-x_3$. Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n7x_1-11(1-x_3)+10x_3 = 3 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad x_1= 2-3x_3\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 - 3x_{3} \\\\ 1 - x_{3} \\\\ x_{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting $x_3=0$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na-3b=2-3\\cdot 1 = -1\n$$" 13194139536031 false] [17592186175767 1145 "First, notice that the Jordan matrix $J$ may be written as a composition of Jordan blocks, as follows:\n\n$$\nJ = J_2(-4) {\\:\\oplus\\:} J_1(3)\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n- a single $2 \\times 2$ Jordan block corresponding to $\\lambda=-4,$ and\n\n- a single $1 \\times 1$ block corresponding to $\\lambda=3.$\n\nTherefore, the columns of $P$ form the following chains: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{2}(-4) & : & {v}_{2}\\overset{A+4I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A+4I\\,}{\\to }0 &  \\\\ {J}_{1}(3) & : & {v}_{3}\\overset{A-3I\\,}{\\to }0 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain that \n\n- the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074075/assets/5477cdf9c4f043da7ae388e93a4fd7bb419e0f8558e0236034324b191dcd8d50.png) of the eigenvalue $\\lambda=-4,$\n\n- the second column of $P$ corresponds to the vector $\\mathbf{v}_2$ such that $(A+4I)\\mathbf{v}_2 = \\mathbf{v}_1.$\n\nNow, computing $A+4I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+4I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -11 