 $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 8 & 0 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+8{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}↔{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we have ${x}_{2}=-\\frac{1}{4}{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n-{x}_{1}+(-\\frac{1}{4}{x}_{3})=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{4}{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{4}{x}_{3} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{4}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{3}=-4,$ we get  \n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{1}{1}=1.$" 13194139536031 true] [17592186380123 1000 #edb/ref 17592186380124 13194139536031 true] [17592186380123 1000 #edb/ref 17592186380125 13194139536031 true] [17592186380123 1000 #edb/ref 17592186380126 13194139536031 true] [17592186380123 1000 #edb/ref 17592186380127 13194139536031 true] [17592186380123 1000 #edb/ref 17592186380128 13194139536031 true] [17592186380123 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139536031 true] [17592186380123 1041 "selection" 13194139536031 true] [17592186380123 1104 #edb/ref 17592186380125 13194139536031 true] [17592186380123 1110 #uuid "a8b6d8d1-5162-539c-8275-e44cc760ee7e" 13194139536031 true] [17592186380124 1034 "\\frac{1}{4}" 13194139536031 true] [17592186380124 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536031 true] [17592186380124 1111 #uuid "2d82aca